{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,165p' src/cljc/yin/vm/values.cljc; rg -n -i 'kernel|oracle' docs/agents/known-gaps.md | head -25; ls /opt/homebrew/bin/clj-kondo /usr/local/bin/clj-kondo 2>/dev/null; tail -15 /private/tmp/h3-run.log\"","aggregated_output":"            (hasheq [_] (hash [:continuation owner payload]))]\n      :cljs [IEquiv\n             (-equiv [_ other]\n                     (and (instance? Continuation other)\n                          (= owner (.-owner ^Continuation other))\n                          (= payload (.-payload ^Continuation other))))\n             IHash\n             (-hash [_] (hash [:continuation owner payload]))\n             IPrintWithWriter\n             (-pr-writer [_ writer _opts]\n                         (-write writer (:continuation marker-text)))\n             Object\n             (toString [_] (:continuation marker-text))]))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :clj (defmethod print-method Closure\n          [_ ^java.io.Writer w]\n          (.write w ^String (:closure marker-text))))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :clj (defmethod print-method Continuation\n          [_ ^java.io.Writer w]\n          (.write w ^String (:continuation marker-text))))\n\n\n(defn owner-tag\n  \"The owner tag of the task whose capability secret is `secret`: derived\n   once, held in VM state, and carried by every closure and continuation\n   the task mints. It reveals nothing of the secret. A task without a\n   secret has owner nil.\"\n  [secret]\n  (when (some? secret)\n    (jing/content-hash [:yin.k/owner secret] {:algorithm :blake3})))\n\n\n(defn closure\n  \"Mint a closure owned by `owner` over the kernel payload map `payload`.\n   A trusted constructor: only kernels call it, never a guest primitive.\"\n  [owner payload]\n  (Closure. owner payload))\n\n\n(defn continuation\n  \"Mint a captured continuation owned by `owner` over the kernel payload\n   map `payload`. A trusted constructor, as `closure`.\"\n  [owner payload]\n  (Continuation. owner payload))\n\n\n(defn closure?\n  \"True when `x` is a closure a kernel minted. A type test: no map is a\n   closure, whatever keys it carries.\"\n  [x]\n  (instance? Closure x))\n\n\n(defn continuation?\n  \"True when `x` is a continuation a kernel captured. A type test.\"\n  [x]\n  (instance? Continuation x))\n\n\n(defn host-typed?\n  \"True when `x` is a closure or a continuation.\"\n58:| Closed 2026-10-24: J2b(a) (§7.8) oracle comparison, JVM canonical (owner 2026-10-24): every host meets the JVM oracle with ZERO pins in `yang.python.antlr.oracle-parity-test` (64 corpus entries, 40 probe texts, the lowering of every ok packet), macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 | J2b run; canonical fixes collab/1792824446211-engineer-canonical-adjudication-fixes.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md; helper patches collab/1792850456065-engineer-j2b-lexer-helper-pins.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md | J2b seat with the yang.antlr host owners | closed: Node/Dart first-failure follow the JVM lazy-buffer rule; manifest patches make the JavaScript helper treat a form feed as a line break and give a DEDENT the last token's line, and make the Dart helper's tokens take the lexer's current line/column (PositionedToken and MakeCommonToken); `bb gen:antlr` changed only Python3LexerBase.js (4724a9da -> 61096183) and Python3LexerBase.dart (e3b11f88 -> 83ad4f38); `yang.python.antlr.cst-export-test` now asserts the JVM's helper coordinates on every host | closed |\n59:| Closed 2026-10-24: J2b(b) restricted lowering comparison: on the fixed corpus (no float, no integer beyond 64 bits, W8), `lower-module-body` of each host's own ok packet equals the JVM's on every host (jolt 35/35 and Node 34/34 and Dart 35/35 by dao.jing content address, the one refusal, `:arith-int64-max` above 2^53 - 1 with no digit budget, identical everywhere), macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 | J2b run, collab/1792793000000-engineer-jolt-j2b-transit-rejoin.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md | J2b seat | recorded by `yang.python.antlr.oracle-parity-test` the-lowering-of-each-ok-packet-is-recorded | closed |\n60:| Closed 2026-10-24: Node and Dart Python completeness probe answered `:complete` for open-bracket input (\"print(\", \"print(\\n\", \"print((1,\", \"f(a for a in b\", \"foo(\\\"\\\"\\\"a\") | J2b oracle-parity run; fix collab/1792824446211-engineer-canonical-adjudication-fixes.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md | yang.antlr host owners | fixed: Node and Dart `cst/first-failure` record the furthest token-stream sync target and use it for a no-viable-alternative error (the JVM lazy-buffer rule); all five answer `:incomplete` and `:bracket-call-open` answers `:end` on both hosts, pins removed | closed |\n62:| jolt Python parser limits surviving J5: no process crash isolation (an uncatchable native fault ends the jolt process); no worker-restart claim; no cancellation of an under-bound parse (time is bounded only through the input and nesting axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL links past the outer context) refuse nesting the JVM parses (nested calls past 408 levels, bracket nests past 453 inside a call, unary runs past 253); time inside the bounds is not bounded by them: the pinned runtime parses a flat left-recursive chain in quadratic time (`1+1+...`: 16,000 operators 7.4 s, 100,000 about 340 s natively on macOS arm64; a 4 MiB chain hours) and an SLL decision whose lookahead walks a `*` chain grows faster (`1*1*...`: 2,000 operators 21 s, 4,000 about 490 s, refused at 4,097 links), so an under-bound source can hold the parse thread for minutes; the guards add up to about 2x on prediction-heavy shapes; a worker freed when no parse thread can be started is leaked, never destroyed on the 